WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.900
 When you walk into the back entrance of Bolter Hall on the UCLA campus, you may notice a

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:16.260
 seemingly random pattern of floor tiles in the entrance.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:20.580
 If you spend a little time looking at the pattern, it might dawn on you that the tiles

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:23.100
 represent zeros and ones.

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:28.500
 And then you might even figure out that the tiles represent ASCII characters.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:35.060
 The characters in the floor tiles spell out "lo" and "behold" to commemorate the building

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:41.580
 where the "L" and the "O" were the first two packets ever sent on the ARPANET from

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:47.460
 UCLA to Stanford Research Institute on October 29, 1969.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:53.840
 ARPA wanted a network so that they could share the large computing resources they had given

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.880
 to their researchers across the country.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:00.380
 University of Utah had a terrific graphics operating system.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:06.940
 SRI had database, we had simulation, University of Illinois had high performance computing.

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:11.700
 And every time ARPA brought on a new researcher, they'd offer to buy them a computer, fine,

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:15.500
 but the researcher would say, "I want the same capability all those other guys have.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.980
 I want the graphics, the database, and all the rest."

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:19.980
 And ARPA said, "We can't afford that."

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.780
 If you want to do graphics, you log on to the machine at Utah through a network that

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:26.700
 we think we're going to make.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.980
 So the need for the network was to do resource sharing and not to protect the United States

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.780
 against a nuclear holocaust.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.460
 When Bob Taylor came in as a neck director, and he recognized his need for sharing resources,

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:43.660
 by the way, notice the phrase I'm using, "sharing resources" is exactly what I built into the

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:44.900
 network design.

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.380
 Now they wanted to share the external resources.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:48.620
 Same idea.

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:51.820
 You have it, you're not using it as somebody else should be able to.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:56.180
 So they brought in Larry Roberts, another classmate of mine, in fact, an officer mate

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:59.580
 of mine at MIT, to manage this project.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.380
 He came to me because he knew my work.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:02.380
 He watched me do the simulation.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:08.740
 In fact, I used his compiler on the TX-2 computer and said, "Len, we need to know if this thing

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:10.500
 is going to work."

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.580
 He knew that I had the theory, so I could show it to him it's going to work.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:18.740
 In fact, he even says he would never have decided to spend millions of dollars of the

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:23.340
 U.S. government's money if he wasn't sure this thing would work.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:28.000
 So the design began to be laid out by a few of us in 1967.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:31.040
 In '68, they sent out a request for a proposal.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:36.700
 The end of '68, both Berenick and Newman, a Cambridge, Massachusetts firm, won the contract

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:40.580
 to produce the first switch of the opener.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:44.660
 And we became the network measurement center early on so we could test it out.

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:49.660
 During the design phase, some great people were there throwing their ideas out.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:53.300
 Herb Baskin was there, a time human expert.

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:58.880
 And he said, "If this network can't deliver short messages within a half a second, I can't

00:02:58.880 --> 00:02:59.880
 use it for time sharing."

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.120
 Specification, half a second.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:08.620
 By the way, we've got 200 milliseconds.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:14.860
 And Wes Clark said, "Circuit computer for communications."

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:18.300
 I said, "Look, if this is going to be an experiment," and I was also interested in the research

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:23.500
 and experimentation, "we have to build software in so we can run experiments.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:27.900
 Artificial traffic generators, measurement hooks, a place where the measurements can

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.300
 be evaluated, put the software in."

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:35.940
 So Howie Frank began to talk about network reliability.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:40.540
 He said, "If anything fails, the network shouldn't collapse."

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:45.300
 So we didn't say there should be five nines of uptime.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:49.700
 We went much more pragmatic, we said, "If any single thing fails, everybody else can

00:03:49.700 --> 00:03:51.220
 still talk."

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:54.500
 So to do that, you need something called a two-connected topology, two independent pairs

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:58.340
 between every pair of nodes, built in it.

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:00.260
 So all those specs went to BBN.

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:01.660
 They built the darn thing.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:05.620
 They delivered the switch here at UCLA on schedule.

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:10.500
 Eight months after they got the contract, they were to deliver this new technology,

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:13.580
 new applications, new device.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:20.180
 They did it on time, on budget, came here, we plugged it in, and bits began to move back

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:25.140
 and forth between our timeshare machine and that switch on the day after Labor Day, September

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:28.660
 2, 1969.

00:04:28.660 --> 00:04:31.340
 But that was just a one-node network.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:35.740
 The schedule was that another one of these switches would be delivered at Stanford Research

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:41.580
 Institute, 400 miles to the north, and they would connect that to their machine.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.780
 And that happened in October.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:47.060
 So in October, we had a two-node network.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:54.700
 My machine, my switch, another switch, 400 miles away, and the SRI host.

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:59.980
 And it wasn't one single line, it was a gang of 4.8 kilobit per second lines.

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:00.980
 So now what do you do?

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:01.980
 You have a two-node network.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:03.500
 Well, now you can do something.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:10.940
 So we decided one night, one night late in October, programmer Charlie Klein and I were

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:15.660
 in the room and said, "Look, let's communicate between these two machines."

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:20.740
 So we got a hold of Bill Duvall, their programmer over there, and we said, "Let's simply log

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:25.260
 in from a terminal connected to our host to that machine."

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:28.340
 The idea is these are both timesharing systems.

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:33.020
 They expect terminals to connect in and use the services of the machine.

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:37.020
 The big thing was sit at a terminal here, log onto your machine here, and through this

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:42.700
 wonderful network, log on here as if you're a local user.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:44.380
 That's easy enough.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:50.180
 So we got all set, got Charlie down at the terminal over here.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:53.460
 And just to be sure this worked, we had a telephone handset.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:57.780
 In fact, I actually think I've got the, here it is, I'm just happy to have it.

00:05:57.780 --> 00:05:58.940
 That was a telephone set.

00:05:58.940 --> 00:05:59.940
 That's the telephone.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:00.940
 That was the telephone.

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:01.940
 We plugged it in.

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:02.940
 We derived a...

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:03.940
 You weren't using Skype.

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:04.940
 Very nice.

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:12.340
 Plugged it in, we used a piece of the high-speed line for the phone connection.

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:18.820
 But the interesting thing is we were using the defunct circuit switching technology to

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:23.740
 prove out the new packet switching technology, and it really helped us so we could understand

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:24.740
 what's going on.

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:27.540
 So Charlie tapped the L and we said, "You get the L?"

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:28.820
 Bill said, "Yep, got the L."

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:31.120
 Tapped the L, "You get the O?"

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:33.340
 We're trying to do LOG for login.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:34.980
 Got the O, got the O.

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:37.580
 Tapped the G, get the G, crash.

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:45.540
 So the first message ever on the network was LO, as in lo and behold.

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:49.540
 Now that's especially interesting because if you go outside this hallway here down under

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:55.260
 the alley and come into another entrance to this building, and I just discovered this about

00:06:55.260 --> 00:07:03.860
 a week ago, you walk on a platform and there's a mosaic of tiles down there, and they're

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:05.940
 a strange pattern.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:10.220
 It turns out it's the ASCII code for lo and behold.

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:12.880
 I have no idea who did that.

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:14.620
 It's about a year and a half now.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:16.740
 Some very clever person put that in.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:17.740
 That was the first message.

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:25.140
 October 29th, 1969, the 1030th night.

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:27.780
 You're entering 1969 right now.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:35.180
 We reproduced this room to look as it did and smell and feel like it did some 40-odd

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:38.180
 years ago.

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:46.060
 If you look over here, you're looking at the first piece of equipment ever on the internet.

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:55.220
 This is that first interface message processor, imp number one at UCLA, a Honeywell minicomputer

00:07:55.220 --> 00:08:01.660
 adapted by both Baranek and Newman, BBNN, to operate as a switch which handles all this

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:03.700
 functionality.

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:11.980
 This is the same physical four square feet where it served as the opening note of the

00:08:11.980 --> 00:08:12.980
 internet.

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:15.620
 It's the first piece of equipment ever on the internet.

00:08:15.620 --> 00:08:16.940
 That's the actual one.

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:17.940
 That's it.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:18.940
 I kept it for years.

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:20.820
 They tried to throw it away many times.

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:23.040
 Most of the people who had imps have tossed them.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:26.140
 This is the one or two left of our coral, but this is number one.

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:27.140
 This is the first piece.

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:34.580
 If you open this machine, you'll be privileged to smell it.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:38.060
 It's got an unusual odor and just brings you right back.

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:39.860
 Emotionally, it's great.

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:41.340
 You can't smell it.

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:44.140
 This is a military-hardened machine.

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:51.260
 This machine was essentially a state-of-the-art minicomputer which was adapted by BBNN.

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:57.420
 I first saw it in 1968 at one of the joint computer conference shows.

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:00.260
 Thousands of people on the big exhibit floor.

00:09:00.260 --> 00:09:02.260
 You see these skyhooks up here?

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:08.900
 They had one of these machines hanging from the ceiling, swinging in the air, running.

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:15.940
 There was a guy, big guy, stripped to the waist, oiled skin with a sledgehammer, and

00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:22.460
 he was going, "Whack, whack," to show that it was a military, and it was.

00:09:22.460 --> 00:09:29.220
 The most important document of the internet, the most important document of the entire

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:32.500
 internet is right here.

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:34.420
 You took out who was working with me.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:37.300
 Well, one of my software programmers was John Postel.

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:45.820
 In fact, here's his picture, and he was not a hippie, even though he appears to be.

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:51.100
 He was the one who basically disciplined my staff to do things properly, keep records.

00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:54.260
 He said, "We have to keep a record of what's going on."

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:59.140
 So beginning in October, basically a month after the imp arrived, we started keeping

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:00.140
 an imp log.

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:01.580
 You know, this is an engineer's log.

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:07.380
 This is not, you know, Madison Avenue piece of data, just scribble, we use an old STS

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:08.380
 log.

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:13.060
 In here, we kept a record of what's going on, and the most important entry happened

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:15.740
 to be right here.

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:22.460
 On October 29, 1969, at 10.30 at night, Charlie Klein, the programmer who was in the room

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:27.740
 with me, made this entry, "Talk to SRI, host to host."

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:35.260
 This is the only record of the very first message ever on the internet, right here.

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:36.900
 You know, we had the technology.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:37.900
 We started making measurements.

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:40.420
 We were the first experimental node.

00:10:40.420 --> 00:10:42.060
 So we saw things happening.

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:45.460
 How come we have a 50 kilobit per second line?

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:48.980
 The routing procedure either goes one way or the other, one at a time.

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:54.300
 So how can you get more than 50 kilobits per second between two nodes if there's only one

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:55.300
 path at any time?

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:58.460
 And we say, "Oh, it's obvious."

00:10:58.460 --> 00:10:59.820
 That path is on now.

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:02.220
 When it gets backed up, you change paths.

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:05.500
 So this guy's empty and it's backed up while you're sending this way.

00:11:05.500 --> 00:11:08.900
 They go close to 100 kilobits per second, right, too.

00:11:08.900 --> 00:11:10.700
 But we could break it as well, as I said.

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:14.980
 Every time we did, we would call BBN and say, "Fix it.

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:15.980
 We did this.

00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:16.980
 Fix it."

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:18.420
 Because they wouldn't give us the code.

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:21.100
 They kept it proprietary until opposite.

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:22.220
 We paid for that code.

00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:23.500
 You have to open it.

00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:24.500
 They did.

00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:25.500
 They saw it.

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:26.500
 Every time we got it...

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:29.380
 And it would take them six months to fix anything.

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:31.400
 We'd discover a fault this time we had the code.

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:33.540
 We'd show them how to fix it.

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:35.740
 Still took six months.

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:39.460
 One of the things I was very much interested in with design was distributed control.

00:11:39.460 --> 00:11:40.460
 Why?

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:45.340
 When I was a student at Shannon, and Shannon's great work came when you had a lot of things

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:46.340
 interacting.

00:11:46.340 --> 00:11:48.420
 Long code words, for example.

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:51.140
 That's when these emerging properties arise.

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:54.300
 So I said, "I want to design large networks.

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:55.300
 Not a large network.

00:11:55.300 --> 00:11:56.900
 You can't have a single point of control.

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:58.260
 You have to distribute it."

00:11:58.260 --> 00:11:59.260
 Right?

00:11:59.260 --> 00:12:01.180
 So what does it mean to distribute control?

00:12:01.180 --> 00:12:05.820
 You're delegating authority to all the peers.

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:09.820
 When ARPA started funding the principal investigators, they had the same philosophy.

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.620
 They said, "Look, you're a smart guy.

00:12:12.620 --> 00:12:13.620
 Here's some money.

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:14.620
 Go do the thing you do best.

00:12:14.620 --> 00:12:16.700
 We're not going to sit on top of you.

00:12:16.700 --> 00:12:17.700
 Make good things happen."

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:20.920
 So here we are, I'm a recipient of that kind of money.

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:21.920
 What do I do with it?

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:24.820
 I've got my graduate students, they're building kids.

00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:26.540
 Look, we need a host-host protocol.

00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:27.540
 Here.

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:31.140
 I'm not going to sit on top of you, I'm going to run with it.

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:34.220
 That is not a product mentality.

00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:37.420
 That is a research, a development, a creative desire, and it worked so well.

